  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 327 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nMain focus remains on the cold front expected to slowly sag through \nthe area late today into Friday, which will serve as focus for \nwidespread rainfall, particularly this evening into Friday morning.\n\nLimited instability will be present in a fairly pseudotropical \nairmass owing largely to the tropical moisture brought into the \nsystem by the remnants of Hurricane Polo in recent days, which will \ninstead present deeply saturated, near moist adiabatic profiles, \npromoting locally heavy downpours as the primary concern.\n\nPrecipitable water values may approach 2 inches at their peak, which \nis near climatological maximum for the time of year, but without a \nmore substantial convective component or stronger forcing, expect \nQPF amounts to be limited to a widespread one half to one inch \nthrough the event, which is well within the capacity of the current \nhydrologic situation per river ensembles and flash flood guidance.\n\nHREF Local Probability Matched Means do indicate an outside chance \nfor isolated areas to approach two inches, but this would be over a \n24 hour period, which again would be of limited impact.\n\nTwo strong areas of cool Canadian high pressure are expected to \ntraverse the Great Lakes in the wake of the cold front this weekend \ninto next week, bringing the coolest air of the Autumn season thus \nfar to central Indiana, along with another prolonged dry spell.\n\nHighs today will again rise well into the 80s across most areas, \nwith lows tonight in the upper 50s to mid 60s, largely modulated by \nlow level humidity, with much cooler conditions thereafter once the \nfront has fully exited the area. Highs from Friday onward will \nlargely fall in the mid 60s to mid 70s, with lows generally in the \n40s to around 50, though early next week the first lows in the 30s \nwill be possible Monday night, mainly northeast.",
